{
  "id": "proj-concrete-kitchen-countertop",
  "slug": "concrete-kitchen-countertop",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Concrete Kitchen Countertop",
  "summary": "A cast-in-place counter with a polished face and integral sink knockout. Heavy, permanent, worth it.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 24,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Check the cabinets can carry it: a 1 1/2 in concrete top weighs close to 19 lb per square foot. Level and shim the cabinets, screw them to each other and into the wall studs, and get the sink maker's cut-out template and the faucet hole size.",
    "Screw 3/4 in plywood to the cabinet tops as the substrate, cut the sink opening in it from the template, and cover it with 6 mil plastic so the concrete does not bond to the wood.",
    "Rip melamine strips so they stand 1 1/2 in above the substrate and screw them around every edge as the side forms. Run a bead of silicone into each inside corner and smooth it with a fingertip; the form tops are your screed rails, so level them with care.",
    "Make the sink knockout: cut rigid foam to the sink template, sand it to exactly the form height, wrap it in packing tape and silicone it to the plastic. Silicone PVC sleeves in place for the faucet holes and trim them to the same height.",
    "Cut the wire mesh 2 in short of every edge and 1 in clear of the knockout, and set it on chairs at mid-depth so it ends up inside the slab, not under it.",
    "Mix the countertop mix in batches at the water ratio on the bag and place it fast, working out from the sink. Consolidate it by running an orbital sander (no paper) along the forms, then screed across the form tops with the straightedge.",
    "Float the surface once the bleed water has gone, then trowel it in several passes as it stiffens over the next few hours, finishing with a hard steel-trowel pass.",
    "Cover it with plastic and keep it damp for at least three days before removing forms or the knockout. Do not load it.",
    "Strip the forms and knockout, ease the edges, then wet-polish the face with the diamond pads, starting at the coarsest grit that removes trowel marks and working up through the set. Fill any pinholes with a slurry of the same mix and polish again.",
    "Let it dry fully, apply the sealer exactly as its label says, and treat it gently until it reaches full strength at about 28 days."
  ],
